* Stash changes to selected files
@code bash
git stash push -m "temporary work" -- path/to/file another/file
git stash list
git stash show -p 'stash@{0}'
git stash apply 'stash@{0}'
@end
`apply` keeps the stash. After reviewing the restored changes, remove it with `git stash drop 'stash@{0}'`.
Add `-u` to `stash push` to include matching untracked files.

* Discard selected unstaged hunks
@code bash
git diff -- path/to/file
git restore -p -- path/to/file
@end
Accepted hunks are replaced with the index version; rejected hunks remain in the working file.

* Recover a commit using the reflog
@code bash
git reflog --date=local
recovered_commit=abc1234 # replace with the hash found above
git show "$recovered_commit"
git branch recovered-work "$recovered_commit"
@end
Creating a branch preserves the commit without changing the current checkout. Reflog entries are local and eventually expire.

* Work on another branch in a separate directory
Run from the original checkout; choose an unused branch name and directory.
@code bash
git worktree add -b fix/example ../project-fix HEAD
git worktree list
@end
After saving or committing the work, remove the checkout from the original directory:
@code bash
git worktree remove ../project-fix
@end
The branch remains. A branch normally cannot be checked out in two worktrees at once.

References: {https://git-scm.com/docs/git-stash}[stash], {https://git-scm.com/docs/git-reflog}[reflog], {https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree}[worktree].
